<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thuringian Highlands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Thuringian Slate Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thuringian Highlands are an upland region of slate hills in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> with the Franconian Forest. Poor in farmland but rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and slate, the area was divided among small lordships, notably the many lines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-reuss">House of Reuss</a>, whose courts sat at towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-lobenstein">Lobenstein</a> and Ebersdorf.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thuringian-highlands.jpg" alt="Thuringian Highlands" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Sander · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schieferhaus_Gehren.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Thuringian Highland, in German the Thüringer Schiefergebirge or Thuringian Slate Hills, is a low range bordering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> to the south-west. It forms a plateau about 20 km wide that slopes south-east towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> valley. Together with the slate hills of the Vogtland it stretches from the Thuringian Forest to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a>, about 75 km from east to west and 50 km from north to south, and forms part of the backbone of the Central Uplands. Its four smaller regions are the upper Saale valley, the Plothen Lake District, the high slate mountains and the Sormitz-Schwarza region.</p><p>The rocks date from the Palaeozoic era, from the Ordovician to the Lower Carboniferous: shale, limestone, sandstone, greywacke and volcanic rocks among them. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>, which formed in a similar way, the region lacks sharp fault lines and passes gradually into the land around it. Cave-forming limestone occurs only in a few small patches, so there are very few caves. Typical of the landscape are wooded dolerite peaks such as the Pöhlde and the Hübel, which stand out because this hard volcanic rock weathers more slowly than the rock around it.</p><p>The rolling hills mostly lie between about 300 m and 500 m, but around the steep valleys of the Schwarza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> the drop from hilltop to valley floor often reaches 300 m or more. The highest summits, in the Sonneberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildburghausen">Hildburghausen</a> districts, include the Großer Farmdenkopf at 869 m, the Kieferle at 867 m and the Bleßberg at 865 m.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The largest towns are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> and Bad Blankenburg on the northern edge, Neuhaus am Rennweg in the highest part and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-lobenstein">Bad Lobenstein</a> in the east, where the hills pass into the Franconian Forest. Two of the largest dams in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> hold back the Hohenwarte and Bleiloch Reservoirs on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, and the Goldisthal Pumped Storage Station in the Schwarza valley, opened in 2003, is one of the largest of its kind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Parts of the area lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringian-forest">Thuringian Forest</a> and Thuringian Highland and Upper Saale Nature Park.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thuringian Highland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ebersdorf was separated from Reuss-Lobenstein in 1678 for Heinrich X, a younger son of the Lobenstein line, creating a small county within the Younger Line of the House of Reuss in the <b>Thuringian Highlands</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-ebersdorf">Principality of Reuss-Ebersdorf</a></li><li><blockquote>Heinrich X received Lobenstein in the <b>Thuringian Highlands</b>, and his descendants later split off Ebersdorf and Hirschberg as further sub-lines.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-lobenstein">Principality of Reuss-Lobenstein</a></li></ul></section>
